  "worked_solution": "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9 & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}({ln}^{2}\u2061(t)+k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{2}\u2061(t)}{t}+\\frac{k}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{ln}^{3}\u2061(t)}{3}+k\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}+k & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
